cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software

title: "DANTE (Domain-Adaptive Network for Transient Evaluation)"
abstract: >
  An open-source Python pipeline for unsupervised morphological
  characterization of LIGO/Virgo gravitational-wave glitch data.
  To overcome the Signal Dilution Limit, the architecture extracts
  dense Patch Tokens from a frozen DINOv2 (ViT) model, applying
  Top-K Multiple Instance Learning (MIL) in the spherical cosine
  similarity space against a Vector-Quantized background reference.
  Classification relies on rigorous Extreme Value Theory (EVT)
  thresholding to guarantee a robust False Positive Rate. The
  extracted anomaly vectors are then grouped via UMAP and Dirichlet
  Process Mixture Model (DPMM) clustering to discover new transient
  morphologies without generative fine-tuning. Version 3 (release 3.6.0)
  adds a multi-scale (0.5-4.0 s) sensitization layer, a block-bootstrap
  Domain Shift Defense, and an empirically calibrated family-wise
  PEM auxiliary-channel coherence veto, replacing raw per-channel
  thresholds and analytic Bonferroni corrections that were found to
  be falsified by direct false-positive-rate measurement. It further
  replaces the embedding-similarity cross-detector coincidence test
  with a physical cross-correlation test over the light-travel lag
  window, whose coherent recovery efficiency is measured across ten
  morphologies. Includes extensive ablation studies and spatial
  saliency mapping. Note that no cross-validation against the Gravity
  Spy catalog was possible: no released Gravity Spy classification set
  covers the O4a epoch.

